     ddcchheecckk checks the consistency of the directory structure of
     each named _f_i_l_e_s_y_s_t_e_m. It makes two passes over the i-list:
     the first walks every directory reachable from the root and
     counts, for every i-number, how many directory entries name
     it; the second compares that count against the link count
     recorded in the i-node itself.

     For every i-node whose entry count does not match its link
     count, ddcchheecckk prints a line giving the i-number, the entry
     count, and the link count (marked (uu) if the i-node is
     otherwise unused, i.e. its mode is zero); if -ss was given,
     it corrects the on-disc link count to match instead of prin-
     ting anything.

     -ss   Instead of reporting bad link counts, repair them: an
          i-node with a nonzero entry count has its link count
          set to that entry count; an i-node with a zero entry
          count and a nonzero mode is cleared (equivalent to
          ccllrrii); an allocated i-node (nonzero mode) whose entry
          count reaches zero only after some directory entries
          pointing to it are themselves cleared during the second
          pass requires a further, third pass, which ddcchheecckk runs
          automatically when needed.  This option requires write
          access to the raw _f_i_l_e_s_y_s_t_e_m.

     -ii _i_n_u_m_b_e_r ...
          While walking the directories, also report every entry
          found whose i-number appears in this list, printing the
          i-number, the containing directory's i-number, and the
          entry name, tagged aarrgg. Up to 20 i-numbers may be
          given.

     Any directory entry whose i-number exceeds the highest valid
     i-number for the file system is reported as bbaadd regardless
     of the -ii list.

     Because ddcchheecckk makes more than one pass over the file sys-
     tem, the _f_i_l_e_s_y_s_t_e_m should be unmounted (or mounted read-
     only) while it runs; use the raw device.  If -ss is used on
     the root file system, the system should be rebooted im-
     mediately afterward, without an intervening ssyynncc.

